# Laravel seed after migrating

You can absolutely execute seeding logic directly within a migration file, but doing so requires understanding exactly when that code runs relative to your database structure and data relationships. The short answer is yes, you can populate tables immediately after creating them, provided you handle the execution context correctly. This capability eliminates the friction of running separate artisan commands just to verify your schema works or to load initial state for local development.

To seed a table within a migration, you leverage the `afterCommit` hook available in Laravel migrations. This method is crucial because it ensures your seeding logic executes only after all changesâincluding schema alterations and data insertsâhave been successfully committed to the database. If an exception occurs during the migration itself, Laravel rolls back the transaction, and the `afterCommit` block will not run, preventing orphaned seed data from polluting a failed state.

```php
use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

return new class extends Migration
{
    public function up(): void
    {
        Schema::create('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('name');
            $table->email('email')->unique();
            $table->timestamp('email_verified_at')->nullable();
            $table->string('password');
            $table->rememberToken();
            $table->timestamps();
        });

        // Create a factory or use static data here
        $users = User::factory(10)->create();

        // This runs only after the migration commits successfully
        User::afterCommit(function () {
            // You can perform additional seeding logic here if needed,
            // such as creating related data or setting specific roles.
        });
    }

    public function down(): void
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('users');
    }
};
```

In the example above, we create the `users` table and immediately instantiate ten users using a factory. While this approach works for simple scenarios, relying on factories inside migrations can sometimes feel brittle if the factory state changes or if you need to manage complex relationships that depend on data already created in subsequent migrations.

A more robust pattern involves separating the schema definition from the data population, even if they happen in the same migration file. You can define a dedicated class for your seeders and call `call()` on it within the migration's `up` method. This keeps your migration logic focused on structure while delegating data concerns to the appropriate tool.

```php
use App\Models\User;
use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Artisan;

return new class extends Migration
{
    public function up(): void
    {
        Schema::create('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            // ... columns
        });

        // Run the seeder command directly from the migration
        Artisan::call('db:seed', [
            '--class' => 'Database\\Seeders\\UserSeeder',
            '--model' => User::class,
        ]);
    }

    public function down(): void
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('users');
    }
};
```

This pattern offers significant flexibility. You can reuse the same seeder class across different environments or even within the same application if you need to run it multiple times with different arguments. It also ensures that your migration file remains readable and maintainable, adhering to the Single Responsibility Principle by not mixing schema creation with complex data generation logic in a single block of code.

However, there are trade-offs to consider. Running seeders inside migrations can make rollbacks slightly more expensive if you are working in a transactional environment that requires strict atomicity across multiple files. Additionally, seeding large datasets directly in a migration might slow down the initial deployment process. For production environments, it is often preferable to run seeders as separate commands after the migration has completed, ensuring that the database schema is ready before data ingestion begins.

If you are looking for streamlined development workflows or need assistance integrating these patterns into your existing stack, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) can provide further insights into optimizing your Laravel application lifecycle. Ultimately, choosing between inline seeding and separate execution depends on your specific project requirements, but having the option to do either within a migration gives you the control needed for efficient development.
